From 2a08a9e320446357a0f3f0421854214b8508b179 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <services.claude@accounts.ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 13:03:02 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: patch 0135 -- discard wraparound-corrupted samples in
 SDL/0058 histo

uclock() (what SDL_GetTicksNS is built on for this backend) is not
monotonic on real DOS hardware -- a wraparound-order subtraction
produces a duration near 2^32 in the underlying unit instead of the
small positive value expected (docs/timing.md 'Your clock can lie').
0058's Shape A scan-timing histo computed
(SDL_GetTicksNS() - _p58_t0) / 1000ULL with no clamping and
accumulated it unconditionally, so a single corrupted sample could
silently inflate noisy_us_total/silent_us_total to an absurd value.

Confirmed in the wild: a real 486DX2-50 capture (dossage/Passage,
SDL_HINT_DOS_SILENCE_HISTO=1) showed noisy_us_total=1271313607
(~21 minutes) over a 54-second run.

Fix: discard a sample outside a generous 1,000,000us (1 second)
sanity ceiling entirely -- neither counted nor accumulated -- rather
than clamping it to that ceiling, per docs/timing.md's own guidance to
treat a wildly-anomalous uclock()-derived duration as a suspect
measurement to discard, not a finding to explain. Clamping instead of
discarding would still corrupt the running average with a fabricated
value. One scan of a caller-supplied audio buffer cannot plausibly
take within three orders of magnitude of a second on any DOS-class
CPU, so the threshold has no realistic false-positive risk against a
genuine sample.

Verified via isolated standalone compile (gcc -Wall -Wextra -Werror,
zero warnings): a synthetic wraparound-underflow delta is discarded,
a normal delta still accumulates correctly.
---
 src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c | 30 ++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c b/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
index 3383715dc..f66645b5f 100644
--- a/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
+++ b/src/audio/dos/SDL_dosaudio_sb.c
@@ -3895,12 +3895,30 @@ static bool DOSSOUNDBLASTER_PlayDevice(SDL_AudioDevice *device, const Uint8 *buf
                    emit below still fires under both shapes). */
                 const Uint64 _p58_us =
                     (SDL_GetTicksNS() - _p58_t0) / 1000ULL;
-                if (all_silence) {
-                    g_p58_silent_count++;
-                    g_p58_silent_us_total += _p58_us;
-                } else {
-                    g_p58_noisy_count++;
-                    g_p58_noisy_us_total += _p58_us;
+                /* SDL/0135: uclock() (what SDL_GetTicksNS is built on
+                   here) is not monotonic on real DOS hardware -- a
+                   wraparound-order subtraction can produce a duration
+                   near 2^32 in the underlying unit instead of the small
+                   positive value expected (docs/timing.md "Your clock
+                   can lie" in the sdl-dos-ports hub). Confirmed in the
+                   wild: an unclamped _p58_us silently inflated
+                   noisy_us_total to an absurd multi-minute total on a
+                   real 486. Per that doc's own guidance, discard a
+                   wildly-anomalous single sample rather than accumulate
+                   or clamp it -- clamping to a ceiling would still
+                   corrupt the running average with a fabricated value.
+                   One scan of a caller-supplied audio buffer cannot
+                   plausibly take within three orders of magnitude of a
+                   second on any DOS-class CPU, so 1,000,000 us is a
+                   generous, still-conservative discard threshold. */
+                if (_p58_us < 1000000ULL) {
+                    if (all_silence) {
+                        g_p58_silent_count++;
+                        g_p58_silent_us_total += _p58_us;
+                    } else {
+                        g_p58_noisy_count++;
+                        g_p58_noisy_us_total += _p58_us;
+                    }
                 }
             }
         }
